          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Regex injection via unescaped glob bracket-set: attacker-controlled `pattern` passed to the public entry point `zipp.Path.glob` (zipp/__init__.py:401, EntryPoint hop_distance=0) flows into `re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern))` at zipp/__init__.py:407. Along the way, `Translator.replace` (zipp/glob.py:59-68) returns `match.group('set') or (re.escape(match.group(0))...)` \u2014 i.e. when `separate()`'s named group `(?P<set>[\\[].*?[\\]])` (zipp/glob.py:106) matches a bracket expression, the raw pattern substring is returned with `re.escape` BYPASSED and spliced verbatim into the compiled regex. Because the `.*?` in the `set` group stops at the FIRST `]`, a pattern whose class ends in a backslash-escaped bracket (e.g. `Path(zf).glob('[a\\\\]')`) injects an unterminated character class into the final regex `(?s:[a\\])\\Z`, changing the regex's structure and raising an uncaught `re.error` out of `glob()` (whose only documented/guarded error is the `ValueError` raised at line 403 for an empty pattern). Callers that validate only `ValueError` crash. Same flow reaches `rglob` (zipp/__init__.py:411-412) via `self.glob(f'**/{pattern}')`.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. zipp/glob.py:63 `return match.group('set') or (re.escape(match.group(0))...)` returns the raw bracket-expression substring with re.escape bypassed, and separate() at glob.py:106 delimits that group with `(?P<set>[\\[].*?[\\]])` whose non-greedy body stops at the FIRST `]`. The unescaped substring is joined at glob.py:57, wrapped as `rf'(?s:{pattern})\\Z'` at glob.py:42, and compiled at zipp/__init__.py:407, so attacker-controlled glob pattern text reaches re.compile verbatim. Reach is graph-confirmed, not an arrow-function artifact: Path.glob (line 401) and Path.rglob (line 411) are both EntryPoint{kind:'handler', exposure:'reachable'} with hop_distance=0, and the calls `tr.translate(pattern)`, `separate(self.star_not_empty(pattern))`, `match.group('set')`, `re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern))` and `self.glob(f'**/{pattern}')` all carry reachable_from_entry=true. Two PoCs follow from CPython's re parser: `glob('[z-a]')` yields `(?s:[z-a])\\Z` -> re.error \"bad character range z-a\" (set branch raises when hi<lo), and `glob('[a\\\\]')` yields `(?s:[a\\])\\Z` where `\\]` is consumed as an escaped bracket, leaving the class open -> re.error \"bad escape \\Z\". re.error subclasses Exception, not ValueError, whereas the module's entire error contract is ValueError (__init__.py:403, glob.py:82, tests/test_path.py:504), so callers guarding ValueError crash. A non-erroring corruption variant also exists: `'[a\\\\]*'` compiles as `(?s:[a\\][^/]*)\\Z`, swallowing the `[^/]*` that `*` translated to into the character class, silently changing which files match. I downgrade severity versus the analyst: all non-class text is unconditionally re.escape'd, so `(`, `)`, `+` and nested quantifiers cannot be injected (no ReDoS beyond ordinary glob), and `prefix = re.escape(self.at)` at __init__.py:405 is escaped so the confinement root cannot be escaped - impact is availability/robustness only, requiring an application to pass an untrusted glob pattern to zipp.Path.glob/rglob. Version pinned as zipp 3.19.0 (NEWS.rst:1); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference evidence was used either way."
          },
