        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The `restrict_rglob` protection at zipp/glob.py:70-82 is an incomplete fix for the glob ReDoS it was written to prevent: it only rejects `**` when mixed *inside* a segment (`'**' in segment and segment != '**'`), so a pattern made of repeated standalone `**` segments such as `**/**/**/**/**/**/x` passes validation and is then translated by `replace()` (glob.py:64) into the chained-wildcard regex `.*/.*/.*/.*/.*/.*/x`, which is compiled at zipp/__init__.py:407 (`matches = re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern)).fullmatch`) and run against every entry name. Chained `.*` separated by literals is the classic catastrophic-backtracking shape, giving super-linear blowup on non-matching long filenames. The exposure is widened by `Path.rglob` (zipp/__init__.py:411-412) which itself prepends a `**/` segment: `return self.glob(f'**/{pattern}')`, so a caller-supplied `rglob('**/**/**/...')` compounds it. The regression tests that would catch exactly this are the `@pytest.mark.flaky`-suppressed ones in lead 1, and the scanner independently ranked this construction as its top dynamic_regex finding.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. restrict_rglob (zipp/glob.py:70-82) raises only when a segment both contains '**' and is not exactly '**' (line 81), so a pattern of repeated standalone '**' segments (e.g. '**/**/**/**/x') passes validation. The in-source doctest at glob.py:53-54 ('**/*' -> '.*/[^/][^/]*') proves that replace() maps each '**' to '.*' and that '/' is left unescaped by re.escape, so '**/**/**/x' translates to '(?s:.*/.*/.*/x)\\Z'. That regex is compiled and run with fullmatch against every zip entry name at zipp/__init__.py:407-409, and the graph confirms that call is reachable (reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1) with Path.glob and Path.rglob both EntryPoints (exposure='reachable'); rglob adds a further '**/' at line 412. Because '.' matches '/' under (?s:), k chained '.*/' groups force the backtracking engine to enumerate increasing k-subsets of the m slash positions in a non-matching name: C(m,k) attempts, e.g. entry ('a/'*30)+'b.txt' with pattern ('**/'*15)+'nomatch' gives C(30,15) ~ 1.55e8 backtracks for a single name, and 60 slashes with 30 '**' gives C(60,30) ~ 1.2e17 (effective hang). k is unbounded. Two sub-claims of the lead are wrong and I corrected them: the premise that restrict_rglob was written as a ReDoS fix is unsupported (no NEWS entry; the message mirrors pathlib's correctness restriction), and the @pytest.mark.flaky complexity tests could not catch this since they all use the fixed benign pattern '*.txt' (tests/test_complexity.py:79-107) and vary zip size only. The core defect -- incomplete pattern validation yielding an attacker-shapeable super-linear regex on a reachable public API -- is real. Severity held to MEDIUM: it is a library DoS that requires an attacker-influenced glob/rglob pattern (attacker control of the zip alone is insufficient, since a fixed '**/*.txt' stays linear). Exploit-catalog grounding was not possible: exploit_search returned 'exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed', so no CVE/version range was compared; tree is v3.19.0 per NEWS.rst:1."
          },
