        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pattern-amplification ReDoS: the regex COMPILED at zipp/__init__.py:407 (`re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern))`) is assembled from the caller-supplied glob `pattern`, and Translator.replace (zipp/glob.py:59-68) expands every `*` into the unbounded quantifier `[^/]*` and every `**` segment into `.*`. A pattern with k stars therefore yields k independent unbounded quantifiers separated by literal text (e.g. glob `*a*a*a*a*a*a*a*a*a*a*z` -> regex `(?s:[^/]*a[^/]*a...[^/]*z)\\Z`). That compiled object is then applied as `.fullmatch` to EVERY member name of the archive (`filter(matches, names)` at line 409, `names` built from `self.root.filelist` at line 408). On a non-matching name of length n, CPython's backtracking engine explores O(n^k) splits, so a short pattern (~40 chars) against ordinary archive names causes super-linear CPU blowup. The only input validation on the path is `if not pattern: raise ValueError` (line 402) and `restrict_rglob` (zipp/glob.py:70-82), which constrains only WHERE `**` may appear, never how many stars a pattern may contain. Re-derive by calling `zipp.Path(z).glob('*a'*20+'*z')` on an archive whose names are long and non-matching.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived. zipp/__init__.py:401-409 compiles a regex assembled from the caller-supplied glob `pattern` (line 407, `re.compile(prefix + tr.translate(pattern)).fullmatch`) and applies it to every archive member name (line 408 `names` from `self.root.filelist`, line 409 `filter(matches, names)`). zipp/glob.py:59-68 `replace()` expands each `*` into the unbounded quantifier `[^{seps}]*` and each `**` into `.*`; glob.py:42 `extend` wraps as `(?s:...)\\Z`. The only guards are `if not pattern` (zipp/__init__.py:402-403) and `restrict_rglob` (glob.py:70-82), which merely rejects `**` that is not a whole path segment -- it blocks ADJACENT stars but places no bound on the NUMBER of stars when separated by a literal or `?`. `star_not_empty` (glob.py:84-94) only rewrites a segment that is exactly `*`, leaving multi-star segments untouched. A grep over all *.py found no pattern-length cap, star-count cap, or regex timeout. CPython's `sre` has no memoization, so k sequential ambiguous quantifiers backtrack combinatorially: `'*a'*20+'*z'` -> `(?s:[^/]*a[^/]*a...[^/]*z)\\Z` costs C(m,20) attempts on a single-segment name with m 'a's and no 'z' (m=40 => ~1.4e11); the stronger `'*?'*20+'z'` -> `(?s:([^/]*[^/]){20}z)\\Z` is maximally ambiguous (both operands accept the same class) and needs NO specific characters, costing ~C(59,19) ~ 1e15 on any ordinary non-matching 60-char filename segment. One correction to the lead's framing: the naive `'*'*k` payload is NOT exploitable (restrict_rglob raises ValueError); the literal/`?` interleaving is required, which the cited payload does satisfy. Reachability is positive, not negative: my own query shows the line-407 `compile` and line-409 `filter` calls, and `Path.glob`/`Path.rglob`, all with reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. The coverage gap is also real: tests/test_complexity.py:79-107 hardcodes the single-star pattern '*.txt' in all three glob complexity tests and varies only archive depth/width, so pattern-side complexity is unguarded. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exploitation requires the embedding application to pass an untrusted glob pattern to Path.glob/rglob. Advisory exploit_search returned 'exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed', and the version is dynamic (setuptools_scm, NEWS.rst tops at v3.19.0) so no patched/unpatched claim is made."
          },
