        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Quadratic backtracking in the `separate()` regex literal at zipp/glob.py:106: `r'([^\\[]+)|(?P<set>[\\[].*?[\\]])|([\\[][^\\]]*$)'` is run with re.finditer directly over the caller-supplied glob pattern. For a pattern consisting of m unmatched `[` characters (e.g. `'['*5000`), at each of the m start positions alternative 1 `([^\\[]+)` fails immediately, alternative 2 `(?P<set>[\\[].*?[\\]])` expands `.*?` all the way to end-of-string looking for a `]` that never appears, and only then alternative 3 matches \u2014 giving O(m^2) character inspections before Path.glob even reaches re.compile. This is polynomial rather than exponential (there is no nested quantifier such as (a+)+ in this literal), so it is a lower-severity amplification than lead 1, but it is pattern-controlled, unguarded, and reached on every Path.glob/Path.rglob call. Re-derive by timing `zipp.Path(z).glob('['*20000)`.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The defect class, location and reachability are real and independently verified, but the analyst's mechanism and repro are wrong and I am correcting them. VERIFIED: zipp/glob.py:106 runs re.finditer(r'([^\\[]+)|(?P<set>[\\[].*?[\\]])|([\\[][^\\]]*$)', pattern) directly over the caller-supplied glob pattern, with no length cap or bracket-balance check (the only validation is `if not pattern` at __init__.py:402 and the `**`-segment rule at glob.py:70-82). Reach path re-derived in real source: Path.glob (__init__.py:401-409) -> tr.translate(pattern) (line 407) -> translate_core (glob.py:31) -> separate(self.star_not_empty(pattern)) (glob.py:57) -> line 106; rglob (411-412) funnels into the same path. CORRECTION: the analyst's stated trigger `'['*m` is NOT quadratic. At pos 0 alt1 fails O(1), alt2 scans ~m chars and fails, then alt3 `[\\[][^\\]]*$` consumes P[1..m-1] and `$` matches at end-of-string, producing ONE match spanning (0,m); finditer then resumes at index m = end, so there are no further start positions. Total cost O(m) -- linear. The analyst's own clause \"and only then alternative 3 matches\" is exactly what prevents the m repetitions their O(m^2) estimate assumes, so `zipp.Path(z).glob('['*20000)` would return in microseconds. A genuine quadratic trigger does exist at the same line, which I constructed myself: for the alternation to fail at a `[` and force finditer to advance one char, alt2 must fail (needs a newline between the `[` and any `]`, since `.` excludes \\n with no DOTALL) AND alt3 must fail (needs a `]` later in the string so `[^\\]]*` cannot reach a `$` position; `$` without MULTILINE matches only at end-of-string or before a FINAL newline). P = '['*m + '\\n' + ']' satisfies both: at each i in 0..m-1, alt2 scans ~(m-i) chars and stalls at the \\n, alt3 consumes to index m+1 where `$` fails on `]` and ~(m-i) backtracks all fail, so the position fails and finditer advances one char -- Sum(m-i) = O(m^2). sre cannot skip positions (alt1's [^\\[] union alt2/3's [\\[] is the universal first-char set) and CPython re has no memoization. Caveats keeping severity LOW: Bash was disabled so this is analytic derivation from documented re semantics rather than measured timing; the amplification is polynomial not exponential; every :EntryPoint in this scan is a test function (tests/test_path.py, tests/test_complexity.py) because zipp is a library, so exploitability requires a downstream consumer to pass attacker-controlled glob patterns to Path.glob. tests/test_complexity.py asserts linear glob complexity but only varies ZIP content (depth/width, lines 80-107), never pattern length, so it does not cover this. exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" and the version is dynamic via setuptools_scm (pyproject.toml:22, NEWS.rst top = v3.19.0), so I could not pin an exact version -- exploit evidence is UNKNOWN and the verdict rests on source analysis alone; the known zipp CVE-2024-5569 is a crafted-zip infinite loop, unrelated to this regex."
          },
